  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 600 PM EDT MON SEP 21 2026\n\nThe models are in generally good agreement with a more blocky \nlong wave pattern to be in place across the CONUS through the \nmajority of the extended portion of the forecast. On Wednesday, an\nupper level ridge center will start out positioned over \nsoutheastern Ontario, while an upper level low will be seen over \nthe Ohio Valley. Meanwhile, further south, a secondary ridge \ncenter will reside over the Ark-La-Tex region. Through the end of\nthe work week, low pressure will win out as it repositions itself\ntowards the Eastern Seaboard. Further west, a ridge axis will \nbecome more prominent across the central CONUS, with deeper \ntroughing ejecting in across the Pacific Northwest. By this \nweekend and into early next week, the upper level low will still \nbe in place across the Eastern Seaboard, although there is more \nnotable model spread by that time at its exact position. The ridge\naxis over the central CONUS will gradually pivot to the east, \nwith the northern extent aligning more over the Great Lakes with \ntime. \n\nSensible weather will feature one more day of better areal\ncoverage of convection across eastern Kentucky on Wednesday, as\nthe upper trough remains close. Better PWATs will be waning by\nthis point; however, depending on how much rainfall occurs\npreceding this day, some locations may be a bit more primed\nleading into it. As such, at least isolated bouts of flash\nflooding can not be ruled out. Given the cloud cover and \nconvective coverage, did lower the highs a bit under the blended \nguidance. This more limited diurnal range idea was kept going \ninto Wednesday night, adjusting lows just a bit higher than the \nblend, as more cloud will likely hold tough, given the pattern. As\nthe trough pulls further east on Thursday, PoPs will be even more\nlimited and confined mainly to our far south and southeast \nlocations. The low levels will more decisively dry out by Friday \nand this dry air continues to remain in place through the weekend \nand into early next week. This will result in pleasant conditions,\nwith highs mainly in the 70s each day, while lows cool off to the\n40s and 50s at night, with peak cooling generally looking like \nthis weekend at this time.",
